# Chroma Library, modern Earth: reflection moments, the character seeing their own shadow (next version, after v22).
# Library thread, 2026-10-09. Design: chroma-ideas/shadows-mechanics.md, sections 5 and 7 ("Seeing it: a reflection
# moment ('I sounded like my father') lowers s[c] by .15 and sets seen[c]"). These follow the 20 shadow moments in
# drafts/next4/earth-shadows.lib and copy its scheme.
# The Engine does the lowering: when one of these moments is met, the shadow part s[c] of the colour in shadow falls by
# .15 and seen[c] is set, whatever the option. The options are only how the character takes what they saw.
# Each moment needs its state (holds:, compiled to the engine's requires). Two per shadow: one for young adult and
# adult life (18-50), one for later life (40-110). Every moment has the five one-colour options and one cross cycle;
# s1 and s2 are used three times each, s3 and s4 twice each.
# Chances, as in earth-shadows.lib: the shadow's own act 40 (pushing the thought away, or doubling down), a neighbour's
# 62, an opposite's 66; cross options by their means colour (own 52, neighbour 58, opposite 62). Neighbours on the
# wheel: W: U and G; U: W and B; B: U and R; R: B and G; G: R and W. Every colour holds each role equally often.
# No colour and no shadow word is named in player text; no option is called wrong.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- rigid (White)

== the old words at the new desk
tier: inner
holds: rigid
stages: young_adult, adult, mature
age: 18-50
tone: mixed
life: work, family
horizon: week
roles: parent, colleague
earth: you hear your parent's exact words come out of your own mouth, at a new starter who made a small slip
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: someone younger working under them; a parent who ruled by rules; a first post in charge of others; much White in the color history with little Red or Black beside it
rarer: working alone; a workplace with no one junior; a parent who was easy-going
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: {N} is telling the new starter off for a small slip, in front of everyone. Halfway through, {N} hears the exact words {parent} used at the kitchen table, the words {N} hated at fourteen.
scene W: {N} only wanted the job done properly. But the voice that came out was not fair, it was hard, and {N} knows the difference.
scene U: {N} replays the sentence on the way home, word for word, and cannot find where good sense ended and the old voice began.
scene B: {N} has always been proud of being nobody's copy. Yet there it was, {parent}, word for word, in {Ns} own mouth.
scene R: {Ns} face burns all afternoon. {N} wants to run back and take every word of it back.
scene G: Some things are handed down without anyone meaning to hand them down. {N} hears the old voice and feels the whole family behind it.
- W | tell yourself standards are standards, and the starter will be grateful one day | v: conformity, security | chance: 40
- U | write down what {parent} used to say, and what you would rather say instead | v: self-direction | chance: 62
- B | decide to be nobody's echo, and work out a way of leading that is your own | v: self-direction, power | chance: 66
- R | find the starter before the end of the day and say sorry, plainly | v: benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 66
- G | ring {parent}, and ask who used to speak to them that way | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 62
- W>U | keep the rule, but write down the reason for it, so nobody needs a telling-off | v: conformity, self-direction | chance: 52
- U>B | study how the best managers correct people, and get better at it than anyone | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 58
- B>R | claim the half day you are owed, and spend it on something that makes you laugh | v: hedonism, self-direction | act: claim the half day they are owed, and spend it on something that makes them laugh | chance: 62
- R>G | own it at lunch, laughing, so the whole team can breathe again | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 62
- G>W | let the hard words go, and keep only the one rule that truly protects people | v: security, benevolence | chance: 58
works: The starter relaxes around {N} within a week, and the work gets better, not worse.
works: The old voice comes again now and then, and {N} catches it before it finishes the sentence.
fails: The starter asks to move to another team, and {N} never quite learns why.
fails: {N} tells it as a funny story, and the old voice goes on speaking anyway.

== the anniversary film at the club
tier: inner
holds: rigid
stages: adult, mature, elder
age: 40-110
tone: mixed
life: friends, leisure
horizon: months
roles: friend
earth: at your club's anniversary dinner, a film of the old years shows you frowning at a notice in every clip
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: long years running a club, a choir, a congregation or a committee; a reputation as the one who keeps order; much White in the color history
rarer: no club or group held for years; someone else in charge
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: At the club's anniversary dinner, someone plays a film of the old years. In clip after clip {N} is frowning and pointing at a notice, while everyone around {N} is laughing. The room laughs fondly. {N} does not.
scene W: {N} kept that club going for twenty years by keeping its rules. On the screen, it looks less like keeping and more like guarding.
scene U: {N} counts, without meaning to: eleven clips, and not one where {N} is smiling.
scene B: {N} always thought of it as running the place. On the screen it looks as if nobody else was ever allowed to.
scene R: {N} wants to laugh along and cannot. Something hot and sad sits in {Ns} throat.
scene G: {N} sees in the frown the one {N} grew up under, and the one before that. It has come a long way down the years.
- W | tell yourself someone had to keep order, and the laughter shows it worked | v: conformity, security | chance: 40
- U | ask {friend} afterwards, honestly, what it was like working beside you | v: self-direction, benevolence | act: ask {friend} afterwards, honestly, what it was like working beside them | chance: 62
- B | hand the chair to someone younger, and keep only the parts you enjoy | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 66
- R | stand up, laugh at the frowning face out loud, and promise the room a sillier year | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 66
- G | let the club's ways change with the people who are in it now | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 62
- W>R | write one new rule: every meeting ends with something fun | v: conformity, hedonism | chance: 52
- U>G | read the club's old minutes, and see how often it changed before your time | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 58
- B>W | use your standing to change the rules so newcomers get a real say | v: power, universalism | chance: 62
- R>U | ring the members who left, and ask them straight out why they went | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 62
- G>B | step back quietly, and keep one small corner of the club as your own | v: security, self-direction | chance: 58
works: At the next meeting {N} lets a rule slide, and nobody notices anything but the warmth.
works: {friend} says, kindly, that it was about time, and the two of them laugh about the frown for years.
fails: {N} changes nothing, and fewer people come to the next anniversary.
fails: {N} tries to loosen up, and the club does not quite believe it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- indecisive (Blue)

== we stopped asking you
tier: inner
holds: indecisive
stages: young_adult, adult, mature
age: 18-50
tone: trouble
life: friends, leisure
horizon: week
roles: friend
earth: your friends planned the weekend away without you, because last time you took three weeks to answer
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: a close group of friends who plan things together; a habit of answering late; much Blue in the color history with little Red or Green beside it
rarer: few friends; living far from the group; a partner who answers for both
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: {N} finds out the weekend away was planned without {N}. {friend} says it plainly, not unkindly: last time they waited three weeks for an answer, so this time they stopped asking.
scene W: {N} meant to be careful with everyone's plans. Instead, everyone's plans waited on {N}.
scene U: {N} was only making sure. Weighing it properly seemed the considerate thing. It never felt like saying no.
scene B: {N} hates being left out of anything. And {N} is the one who handed the choice to someone else, week after week.
scene R: It stings like a slap. {N} would have said yes, in the end.
scene G: {N} has always waited for things to ripen. It turns out the others got tired of waiting.
- U | explain to {friend} why each of those choices needed thinking over | v: self-direction, security | chance: 40
- W | promise {friend} an answer within a day from now on, and keep to it | v: conformity, benevolence | chance: 62
- B | choose the next trip yourself, book it, and invite them along | v: self-direction, power | chance: 62
- R | ask to come along anyway, and say yes before {friend} finishes the sentence | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 66
- G | take the hint, and say yes to the next small thing without weighing it | v: benevolence, security | chance: 66
- W>B | set a firm rule of your own: two days to decide anything, then stand by it | v: self-direction, conformity | chance: 58
- U>R | flip a coin for the next small choice, and go wherever it lands | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 52
- B>G | offer to organise the next weekend for everyone, somewhere you all know | v: benevolence, achievement | chance: 58
- R>W | tell {friend} straight that it hurt, and that they were fair to stop asking | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 62
- G>U | watch how {friend} decides things so easily, and learn from it slowly | v: self-direction, tradition | chance: 62
works: Next time {friend} asks, {N} says yes on the spot, and has a better time than any plan could have promised.
works: The invitations come back, slowly, and {N} answers them by return.
fails: {N} starts a list of reasons, and the next plan is made without {N} too.
fails: {N} says yes too fast to the wrong thing, and misses the old caution.

== five notebooks, one list
tier: inner
holds: indecisive
stages: adult, mature, elder
age: 40-110
tone: mixed
life: dreams, home
horizon: months
roles: friend
earth: clearing a drawer, you find five notebooks from five times of life, each with the same list of reasons to go and to stay
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: a long-held wish to move, retrain or travel; a habit of writing lists; much Blue in the color history
rarer: a life already full of moves; no papers kept
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: Clearing a drawer, {N} finds five notebooks from five different times of life. Each holds the same list: reasons to go, reasons to stay. The handwriting changes. The list does not.
scene W: {N} always wanted to choose properly, with nothing left out. Five times, nothing was chosen at all.
scene U: {N} reads the lists as if they were someone else's work, and sees at once what that person never saw: there was never going to be enough to go on.
scene B: Every one of those years, someone else took the chance {N} was weighing. {N} can name some of them.
scene R: Thirty years of wanting to go, all on paper. {Ns} hands are shaking a little.
scene G: The seasons turned five times while the list sat in the drawer. {N} feels the weight of all that waiting.
- U | start a sixth list, more careful than the others, to see what was missed | v: self-direction | chance: 40
- W | show the notebooks to {friend}, and ask to be held to a decision by spring | v: conformity, security | chance: 62
- B | pick the one wish still worth having, and pay for it this month | v: self-direction, hedonism | chance: 62
- R | tear out the pages, book a short trip somewhere new, and go next week | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | chance: 66
- G | keep the notebooks as a record of who you were, and make peace with staying | v: tradition, security | mark: stayed home | chance: 66
- W>G | promise to visit the one place that is on every list, before the year is out | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 58
- U>W | write down what the lists taught you, for a younger friend facing the same choice | v: universalism, benevolence | act: write down what the lists taught them, for a younger friend facing the same choice | chance: 52
- B>U | sign up for the course weighed in the third notebook, and pay up front | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | chance: 58
- R>B | sell something kept just in case, and spend the money on going | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 62
- G>R | sit by the river with the notebooks, and see what you feel without thinking | v: hedonism, self-direction | chance: 62
works: {N} chooses something, small but real, and the drawer stays shut after that.
works: {N} goes, or stays, and for once it feels like a choice.
fails: The notebooks go back in the drawer, and a sixth one joins them a year or two later.
fails: {N} rushes into the first thing on the list, and it is not the thing {N} wanted.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- ruthless (Black)

== learned from the best
tier: inner
holds: ruthless
stages: young_adult, adult, mature
age: 18-50
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: colleague
earth: a younger colleague you trained takes the account you wanted, using exactly the trick you taught them
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: a sales or deal-making job; someone younger trained by them; much Black in the color history with little White or Green beside it
rarer: working alone; a team that shares credit by habit
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: {colleague}, whom {N} trained, takes the account {N} wanted by going round {N} to the client the night before. {colleague} grins. It is exactly what {N} taught them. "I learned from the best."
scene W: {N} feels the unfairness of it like a blow. Then, slowly, {N} remembers all the times it was done the other way round.
scene U: {N} can trace every move back to a lesson {N} gave. It is like reading {Ns} own handwriting in someone else's hand.
scene B: Part of {N} is proud. The rest of {N} is seeing, for the first time, how it looks from the other side of the table.
scene R: {N} wants to shout. Instead {N} stands very still, because {N} would have done the same, and said the same.
scene G: What one plants comes up. {N} always knew that about gardens, and is only now seeing it about people.
- B | shrug it off: that is the game, and next time you will play it better | v: power, achievement | chance: 40
- W | tell {colleague} you have both played it unfairly, and agree on rules for the team | v: universalism, conformity | act: tell {colleague} they have both played it unfairly, and agree on rules for the team | chance: 66
- U | go back through the deals you won, and see who paid for each one | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 62
- R | laugh, take {colleague} out for a drink, and say what it felt like | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 62
- G | hand the next client back to the colleague you once took them from | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 66
- W>R | propose that the team shares every bonus, then celebrate the next win together | v: universalism, hedonism | chance: 62
- U>G | work out a way of training newcomers that builds trust, not tricks | v: benevolence, self-direction | chance: 58
- B>W | use your seniority to set an honest rule on clients, and keep it first yourself | v: power, conformity | chance: 52
- R>U | ask {colleague} straight out which of your habits they picked up, and listen | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 58
- G>B | step back from the chase for a season, and let your work stand on its own | v: security, self-direction | chance: 62
works: {N} and {colleague} end up the strongest pair in the office, and both of them play straighter.
works: Clients start asking for {N} by name, for reasons {N} is quietly prouder of.
fails: {N} learns nothing from it, and the next young hire learns everything.
fails: The tricks stop, but the trust does not come back, and {colleague} moves to a rival firm.

== the birthday toast
tier: inner
holds: ruthless
stages: adult, mature, elder
age: 40-110
tone: trouble
life: family, money
horizon: months
roles: sibling
earth: at your birthday dinner, your sibling raises a glass and says you always got what you wanted, and never gave anything back
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: a sibling who gave things up over the years; money or a house gained at family cost; much Black in the color history
rarer: no siblings; a family that never gathers
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: At {Ns} birthday dinner, {sibling} stands to give a toast, a glass too many in. "{N} always got what {N} wanted," {sibling} says, smiling. "I am still waiting to get something back." Nobody laughs.
scene W: {N} paid for the dinner, as {N} pays for most things. It never struck {N} that paying is not the same as giving.
scene U: {N} starts, without meaning to, a list of what {sibling} lent, gave or gave up over the years. It is a long list.
scene B: {N} always believed people get what they fight for. Looking round the table, {N} sees who was never allowed to fight.
scene R: {N} wants to snap back, and the words die. {Ns} face is hot and {Ns} hands are cold.
scene G: Old debts have a way of rising at family tables. {N} feels this one come up through the floor.
- B | raise your glass, make a joke of it, and move the evening on | v: power, security | chance: 40
- W | ask {sibling} for a proper talk, and repay what is owed, in full | v: universalism, conformity | mark: owned up | chance: 66
- U | sit with {sibling} the next day and go through it, honestly, year by year | v: self-direction, benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 62
- R | stand up there and then, and tell {sibling} they have a point | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 62
- G | let the words stand, and spend the next year simply being around more | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 66
- W>U | write {sibling} a letter that names every debt, and learn what each one cost them | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 62
- U>B | work out what {sibling} most needs now, and use your weight to get it for them | v: benevolence, power | chance: 58
- B>R | take {sibling} on the trip they always wanted, and pay for every part of it | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 52
- R>G | turn up at {sibling}'s door with food every Sunday, unasked | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 58
- G>W | quietly change the will so it is fair to everyone who gave | v: universalism, benevolence | chance: 62
works: {sibling} rings the next week, and for the first time in years they talk about nothing in particular.
works: The debt is never quite settled, but it stops being the only thing between them.
fails: {sibling} apologises for the toast, and nothing more is ever said about it.
fails: {N} tries to put it right with money, and {sibling} sends the money back.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- reckless (Red)

== the video from the weekend
tier: inner
holds: reckless
stages: young_adult, adult, mature
age: 18-50
tone: trouble
life: friends, health
horizon: week
roles: friend
earth: a friend shows you a video from the weekend, and you barely recognise the person shouting in it
requires: holds the state (force above .5) for six months or more; at most once every three years for this shadow
likelier: big nights out; friends who film everything; much Red in the color history with little White or Blue beside it
rarer: a quiet social life; friends who keep their phones away
times: Some people who stay in the state, about one in four of them over three years (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: {friend} shows {N} a video from the weekend. On the small screen {N} is shouting at a doorman, then telling {friend} to get lost. {N} remembers almost none of it, and {friend} is not smiling.
scene W: {N} would never let anyone talk to a friend like that. Watching it is like watching a stranger break a promise.
scene U: {N} watches it twice, looking for the moment it turned. There is no such moment. It was heading there all along.
scene B: {N} likes to think nobody tells {N} what to do. On the screen, something else is plainly in charge.
scene R: {N} always says that is just how {N} is: all in. The face on the screen does not look free. It looks lost.
scene G: {N} sees an uncle in the face on the screen, the one the family never talked about.
- R | laugh it off: everyone was wild that night, and life is short | v: hedonism, stimulation | chance: 40
- W | apologise to {friend} properly, and promise to stay sober next time out | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 66
- U | count how many weekends ended like this, and what each one cost | v: self-direction, security | chance: 66
- B | decide that from now on your nights run on your terms, not the drink's | v: self-direction, power | chance: 62
- G | spend the next few weekends at home, cooking for friends, and see how it feels | v: benevolence, hedonism | chance: 62
- W>G | ask {friend} to keep you to quieter nights, the way old friends do | v: benevolence, tradition | act: ask {friend} to keep them to quieter nights, the way old friends do | chance: 62
- U>W | read up on what drink does to judgement, and set fair limits for the group | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 62
- B>U | delete the video, and learn the signs that a night is about to turn | v: self-direction, security | chance: 58
- R>B | pour the same fire into training for something hard, and win it | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 52
- G>R | go camping with {friend}, far from any bar, and be wild the old way | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 58
works: A month later {friend} watches the video again with {N}, and this time they both laugh.
works: The weekends get quieter and, to {Ns} surprise, better.
fails: The video goes round the group, and {N} spends the next night out proving it was a joke.
fails: {friend} accepts the apology, and stops inviting {N} anyway.

